1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Wang Xi 王錫 (499–534), ''zi'' Gongjia 公嘏. Liang period writer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wang Xi's ancestral home was Linyi 臨沂 in Langye 琅邪 commandery (modern Linyi, Shandong). His father Wang Lin 王琳 was married to the Princess of Yixing, Xiao Lingyi 蕭令嫕, the younger sister of Xiao Yan 蕭衍 (464–549), Emperor Wu (r. 502
2 CONC –549) of Liang. As a child of seven or eight he visited his mother in the imperial palace where he made a fine impression on Emperor Wu. As a youth he was quite intelligent and was a diligent student. At the age of twelve he becam
2 CONC e a student at the national university, and at age fourteen he was appointed assistant in the palace library. Wang Xi and Zhang Zuan 張纘 (499–549), who was another prodigy, were assigned to the staff of the crown prince Xiao Tong 蕭
2 CONC 統 (501–531). Emperor Wu ordered them to serve as the prince's mentors and companions. 
2 CONT
2 CONT About this time Wang Xi was granted the noble title of Marquis of Yong'an. He was also assigned as companion for the Prince of Jin'an, Xiao Gang 蕭綱 (503–551), but Wang declined the post on the grounds of illness. in January 521, t
2 CONC he Northern Wei court sent Liu Shanming 劉善明 as an envoy to the Liang court. Liu Shanming requested to meet Wang Xi and Zhang Zuan. Liu was much impressed with their knowledge of the Classics and histories, as well as their wit. In
2 CONC  522, Wang Xi was promoted to director of the Bureau of Personnel. Because he was a member of the consort clan, he received many honorific titles, which he was not able to accept because of a chronic illness. Wang Xi dismissed his
2 CONC  attendants and refused to receive guests. He died in 534 at the age of thirty-six. The monographs on bibliography of the ''Sui shu'' and the two Tang histories list Wang Xi's collected works in seventeen ''juan''. This was lost i
2 CONC n the Song. His only extant works are “Sushan si fu” 宿山寺賦 (''Fu'' on the Sushan Monastery), and two poems that he composed to match pieces by Xiao Tong.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography
2 CONT
2 CONT Studies
2 CONT
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng and Shen Yucheng, Zhongguo wenxuejia dacidian, 33. 
2 CONT * Cao Daoheng 曹道衡 and Fu Gang 傅剛. Xiao Tong pingzhuan 蕭統評傳, 172. Nanjing: Nanjing daxue chubanshe, 2001.
2 CONT
2 CONT Works
2 CONT
2 CONT a. “Dayan shi” 大言 (Magniloquence) and “Xiyan shi” 細言詩 (Diminiloquence)
2 CONT
2 CONT Translations
2 CONT
2 CONT * Wang Ping. “Culture and Literature in an Early Medieval Court: The Writings and Literary Thought of Xiao Tong (501–531.” Ph.D. dissertation, University of Washington, 2006, 19. 
2 CONT * Wang Ping. The Age of Courtly Writing, 29.
2 CONT
2 CONT DRK
